==French==

===Etymology===
{{lit|look there}}. From {{m|fr|vois||see!, look!}}, second-person singular imperative of {{m|fr|voir||to see, to look}} and {{m|fr|là||there}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{fr-IPA}}
* {{audio|fr|LL-Q150 (fra)-Lyokoï-voilà.wav|Audio}}
* {{homophones|fr|voila|voilât}}

===Verb===
{{fr-verb}} ([[defective]])

# [[there]] (it) is
#* '''1953''', {{w|Samuel Beckett}}, ''En attendant Godot''
#*: {{quote|fr|'''Voilà''' l’homme tout entier, s’en prenant à sa chaussure alors que c’est son pied le coupable.|'''There is''' man in his entirety, blaming his shoe when his foot is guilty.}}
# [[here]] (it) is
#: {{ux|fr|'''Voilà''' le fromage.|'''Here's''' the cheese.}}
#: {{ux|fr|'''Voilà''' ce qu'on va faire ensuite.|'''Here's''' what we are going to do next.}}
# [[that]] is
#: {{ux|fr|'''Voilà''' ce que je lui ai demandé, et voici sa réponse : « ... »|'''That's''' what I asked her and this is her answer: "..."}}

====Usage notes====
* ''voilà'' is a [[defective]] verb. Its only conjugation is in the present indicative tense, even though it can appear in phrases that imply another tense.
* It is mainly used to introduce a slightly distant person or object, in contrast to {{m|fr|voici}} which is used to designate a person or object near the speaker.
* In face-to-face conversations where both participants can see the subject of the conversation, ''voilà'' often supersedes {{m|fr|voici}} (thus its additional definition: ''Here is'').

====Derived terms====
* {{l|fr|en veux-tu en voilà}}
* {{l|fr|et voilà le travail}}
* {{l|fr|voilà qui est fait}}
* {{l|fr|voilà tout}}

